{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tUBTuDFCmnHv7SbaZUf9w","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Decisions: Android home-screen widgets\n2\t\n3\tdate: 2026-10-29 (revised the same day, see §1)\n4\tbuilds on: `docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md` (2026-10-15)\n5\tscope: architecture decisions for the widget foundation. No code was\n6\tchanged. The first widget is \"Your Day\", but these decisions are meant to\n7\tcarry many more widgets, including interactive ones (tap-to-complete,\n8\tstart/stop timer). The goal is a solid foundation: no choice here should\n9\tneed revisiting when the next widget or the next interaction arrives.\n10\t\n11\t## 1. Summary\n12\t\n13\tThe report recommended a \"web app pushes a snapshot\" design, because it\n14\tavoided both the token problem and backend changes. Two facts change that:\n15\tinteractive widgets are a real goal, and the backend can change.\n16\t\n17\tA second pass checked the first version of this document against the code\n18\tin `api/`, `app/` and `android/` and with tap-to-complete in scope from the\n19\tstart. It changed D1, D2, D6, D7, D8 and D9, dropped D10 (token rotation) and\n20\tadded D11–D13.\n21\t\n22\t| # | Decision |\n23\t|---|---|\n24\t| D1 | Native fetches widget data with its **own widget token**, bound to the session token that issued it and only valid on widget routes |\n25\t| D2 | The **backend builds widget data** in one generic, display-ready section/row format |\n26\t| D3 | Native code is **Java + `AppWidgetProvider`/`RemoteViews`**; Glance rejected |\n27\t| D4 | Widgets **show details**; no privacy mode |\n28\t| D5 | **No Capacitor upgrade** is needed for widgets |\n29\t| D6 | Widget taps open routes through the bridge (D7); route keys are mapped to paths in `../app` |\n30\t| D7 | The JS ↔ native bridge uses **`addWebMessageListener`**, restricted to the app's origin |\n31\t| D8 | **Tap-to-complete is part of the foundation**: generic action plumbing, first used by Your Day |\n32\t| D9 | Freshness: periodic fetch, server-given `refresh_at`, refresh after changes in the app |\n33\t| D10 | *(dropped: token lifetime and rotation now follow from D1)* |\n34\t| D11 | One fixed **error contract** between widget routes and native |\n35\t| D12 | **Self-hosted and outdated servers** are handled explicitly |\n36\t| D13 | **Prerequisite:** logout in `../app` revokes the token on the server |\n37\t\n38\t## 2. Findings that shaped the revision\n39\t\n40\tThese were checked in the code on 2026-10-29.\n41\t\n42\t- **Logout never revokes the token on the server.** `Auth.logout()`\n43\t  (`app/src/lib/state/Auth.svelte.ts`) only clears localStorage, and no code\n44\t  calls `POST /auth/logout`. A logged-out token stays valid until it expires\n45\t  (up to 90 days), on every platform. See D13.\n46\t- **`addJavascriptInterface` is visible to every frame**, third-party ones\n47\t  included. The app embeds a Deezer iframe (`DeezerSneakPreview.svelte`).\n48\t  See D7.\n49\t- **Sanctum abilities are not checked anywhere.** Every protected route is\n50\t  behind plain `auth:sanctum` (`api/routes/api.php`), which accepts any valid\n51\t  token. A token with only a `widget` ability would still reach every\n52\t  endpoint. See D1.\n53\t- **The app refreshes its token by itself**: on start, once the token is\n54\t  within 14 days of expiry on native (`shouldRefresh`). The refresh deletes\n55\t  the old token (`AuthService::refreshToken`). See D1.\n56\t- **Mobile users can set their own API URL** (`getCustomApiUrl`), so the\n57\t  server may be older than the app. See D12.\n58\t- **Todo due dates are dates, not timestamps** (`Todo::$casts`,\n59\t  `due_at => date`), and so are all-day events. See D2.\n60\t- **Completing a recurring todo creates the next occurrence**\n61\t  (`TodoService::update`). See D8.\n62\t\n63\t## 3. Decisions\n64\t\n65\t### D1: a widget token bound to its session\n66\t\n67\t- **Issuing:** `POST /auth/widget-token`, called by the web app with its\n68\t  normal token. It creates a Sanctum token named `widget-token` with the\n69\t  ability `widget` and returns it with its expiry. The route must **not** be\n70\t  named `widgets.*` (see the gate below).\n71\t- **Bound to the parent:** a new nullable column `parent_token_id` on\n72\t  `personal_access_tokens`, a foreign key to the issuing token with\n73\t  `ON DELETE CASCADE`, and a **unique** index. Issuing replaces any existing\n74\t  widget token of the same parent in one transaction. So:\n75\t  - there is at most one widget token per session, enforced by the database;\n76\t  - whatever ends a session (logout, revoking it in the session list,\n77\t    `logout-all`, a password change, account deletion, a token refresh)\n78\t    deletes its widget token too, with no extra code paths;\n79\t  - a lost phone is handled by revoking that phone's session, which the user\n80\t    would do anyway.\n81\t- **Lifetime:** the widget token's `expires_at` is the parent's. A widget\n82\t  token can never outlive its session. There is no separate rotation\n83\t  endpoint: every app token refresh deletes the old widget token (cascade),\n84\t  and the app issues a new one right after (D7). The widget secret therefore\n85\t  rotates with the session.\n86\t- **Scope: one central gate, deny by default.** In\n87\t  `Sanctum::authenticateAccessTokensUsing`, a token without the `*` ability\n88\t  is valid only on routes named `widgets.*`. Routes added later anywhere are\n89\t  closed to widget tokens automatically. Normal tokens (`*`) may call widget\n90\t  routes too.\n91\t- **Session list:** `GET /auth/tokens` does not list widget tokens. They\n92\t  belong to their session, and revoking the session covers them. The app's\n93\t  session UI does not change.\n94\t- **At rest on the device:** an AES-GCM key in the **AndroidKeyStore**\n95\t  encrypts the token. The ciphertext, IV, expiry and API URL go into private\n96\t  `SharedPreferences`, excluded from backup (`fullBackupContent` ≤ API 30,\n97\t  `dataExtractionRules` API 31+). That is a manifest change, so flag it in\n98\t  review. `androidx.security:security-crypto` is deprecated; do not use it.\n99\t- Native **never sees the WebView's token**, and never returns the widget\n100\t  token to JS.\n101\t\n102\t**Why:** interactive widgets need server writes from outside the app. A\n103\tseparate token keeps the main credential in one place. Binding it to the\n104\tsession makes revocation impossible to forget or skip, without new revoke\n105\tendpoints or client-side bookkeeping.\n106\t\n107\t### D2: the backend builds display-ready widget data\n108\t\n109\t- One endpoint per widget. The first is `GET /widgets/your-day`, used only\n110\t  by the widget for now. The app's own dashboard Your Day does not change.\n111\t- Your Day content, in this order: **today's events**, **due and overdue\n112\t  todos**, **upcoming events** (the 3-day range `listWidgetEvents` already\n113\t  uses). The \"scored todos\" part of the dashboard stays out: its scoring\n114\t  only exists in TypeScript.\n115\t- One **generic format** shared by all widgets:\n116\t\n117\t  ```\n118\t  {\n119\t    kind, schema_version, generated_at,\n120\t    refresh_at,                         // when this data goes stale (next local midnight)\n121\t    labels: { ... },                    // pre-translated UI strings (title, empty state, ...)\n122\t    sections: [{\n123\t      key, title?,\n124\t      rows: [{\n125\t        id, title, subtitle?, time_label?,  // display-ready text\n126\t        at?, until?, all_day,               // raw values: ISO timestamps, or dates when all_day\n127\t        icon?,                              // key from a fixed icon set, never a URL\n128\t        route: { key, id? },                // mapped to a path by ../app (D6)\n129\t        action?: { type, key, checked? }    // D8\n130\t      }]\n131\t    }]\n132\t  }\n133\t  ```\n134\t\n135\t  - The server knows the user's timezone, language, 12/24-hour and date\n136\t    format, so it produces the text. Native only renders it and cannot end\n137\t    up inconsistent with the app.\n138\t  - Raw values stay for ordering and future use. Dates are never sent as\n139\t    UTC timestamps.\n140\t  - `refresh_at` tells native when to fetch again, so native needs no\n141\t    timezone logic.\n142\t  - **Versioning:** additive changes keep `schema_version`. Native ignores\n143\t    fields, section types and action types it does not know. A higher\n144\t    major version than native supports shows \"update the app\".\n145\t  - The server caps rows per section; the job sets the number.\n146\t- A new widget is mostly a backend endpoint plus a native layout, with no\n147\t  logic rewritten in Java.\n148\t\n149\t**Why:** the API serves every kind of client. Building the data on the\n150\tserver keeps business logic out of Java, and the WebView, the widgets and\n151\tany future client can read the same data.\n152\t\n153\t**Note (optional, not part of this work):** the \"today/overdue\" logic then\n154\texists both in `TodoFilterService` (TypeScript) and on the server. The\n155\tdashboard's `YourDay.svelte` could later read the same endpoint.\n156\t\n157\t### D3: Java + `RemoteViews`\n158\t\n159\t- The native code is written by AI, so less code per widget is not a deciding\n160\t  factor. What is left favours the platform API:\n161\t  - **No new build tooling.** Glance would bring Kotlin, the Compose compiler\n162\t    and Glance itself, whose versions must stay compatible with each other\n163\t    and with AGP.\n164\t  - **No abstraction layer.** `RemoteViews` is what the launcher renders.\n165\t  - **No F-Droid risk** and no APK growth (release builds run with\n166\t    `minifyEnabled false`).\n167\t  - **One language** in `android/`, matching `MainActivity` and the plugins.\n168\t- Allowed new dependencies, all Apache-2.0, from Google Maven, with no GMS:\n169\t  `androidx.core:core-remoteviews` (list widgets on minSdk 24) and\n170\t  `androidx.work:work-runtime` (D9). `androidx.webkit` is already present\n171\t  (D7). Networking uses the platform (`HttpURLConnection` + `org.json`), with\n172\t  no OkHttp/Retrofit.\n173\t- **Intents:** row clicks in collection widgets need a **mutable** template\n174\t  `PendingIntent` (with an explicit component) for the per-row fill-in\n175\t  intents; Android requires this on API 31+. All other widget\n176\t  `PendingIntent`s are explicit and `FLAG_IMMUTABLE`, with a distinct request\n177\t  code or data URI each. Receivers treat fill-in extras as untrusted and\n178\t  validate the key and id format.\n179\t- API levels: checkboxes (`setCompoundButtonChecked`) need API 31. On\n180\t  24–30, fall back to a tappable icon (report §3.1).\n181\t\n182\t### D4: widgets show details\n183\t\n184\tPlacing a widget on the home screen is the user's own choice, and so is what\n185\tit shows. No \"hide details\" mode and no setup screen for now. A setup screen\n186\tcan be added later when a widget needs per-widget options.\n187\t\n188\t### D5: no Capacitor upgrade for widgets\n189\t\n190\tNothing here depends on a Capacitor plugin. The bridge is our own (D7), and\n191\twidgets use platform APIs.\n192\t\n193\t### D6: widget taps open routes through the bridge\n194\t\n195\t- Rows carry `route: { key, id? }`, for example `{ key: \"todo\", id: \"…\" }`.\n196\t  **`../app` owns the mapping** from keys to paths and validates the id\n197\t  format. Native keeps no list of paths.\n198\t- `MainActivity` handles widget intents in `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` and\n199\t  only checks the key's format:\n200\t  - **app ready** (warm start): native sends `navigate { key, id? }` over the\n201\t    bridge, and the app calls SvelteKit's `goto()`. An instant switch, with\n202\t    app state kept.\n203\t  - **not ready** (cold start): native keeps the pending route and sends it\n204\t    when the app reports `ready`.\n205\t- The app ignores unknown keys and invalid ids. If the user is not logged\n206\t  in, the normal login redirect applies.\n207\t- `MainActivity` is `exported=\"true\"`, so any app can send it an intent. The\n208\t  worst a forged intent can do is open a known page.\n209\t- Moving share (`handleShareIntent`) to the bridge is possible later but is\n210\t  out of scope.\n211\t\n212\t**Why:** a full `loadUrl` throws away the running app on every tap. One\n213\tchannel for everything avoids a global JS function and building JS strings\n214\tfor `evaluateJavascript`.\n215\t\n216\t### D7: the JS ↔ native bridge\n217\t\n218\t- Mechanism: **`WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener`** with the allowed\n219\t  origin `https://my.solyto.app`. Only that origin's frames get the object,\n220\t  each message carries its source origin and whether it came from the main\n221\t  frame (native accepts main-frame messages only), and native can reply on\n222\t  the same channel. If the WebView lacks the feature, widgets show \"not\n223\t  supported on this device\" and the app carries on.\n224\t- Messages from the app:\n225\t  - `ready`: the app is running. Native replies `{ needsToken }` (true when\n226\t    it has no token or the stored one has expired) and delivers a pending\n227\t    route (D6).\n228\t  - `setToken { token, expiresAt, apiUrl }`\n229\t  - `clear`: logout. Native deletes the token and cached widget data and\n230\t    shows the signed-out state.\n231\t  - `refresh`: data a widget shows has changed (debounced in JS).\n232\t  - `unsupported`: the app's server has no widget routes (D12). Native drops\n233\t    any token and cached data and shows \"server doesn't support widgets\".\n234\t- Messages from native: `navigate { key, id? }`.\n235\t- When the app requests a widget token: after `ready` when native replies\n236\t  `needsToken`, after login, and **after every successful token refresh**\n237\t  (D1).\n238\t- Logout order: `clear` first, then `POST /auth/logout` (D13).\n239\t- In `../app`, all bridge use goes through one `IS_MOBILE`-gated module that\n240\t  checks whether the bridge object exists, so web and desktop are\n241\t  unaffected.\n242\t\n243\t### D8: interactions (part of the foundation)\n244\t\n245\t- A row action is `{ type, key, checked? }`. Native knows only the UI\n246\t  **types** (`check` for now). The **key** names the server action, for\n247\t  example `todo.complete`. Native validates the key format and posts to\n248\t  `POST /widgets/actions/{key}/{id}`.\n249\t- Backend: an **explicit route per action key**, never a generic\n250\t  dispatcher. Each checks ownership the same way the normal endpoints do\n251\t  and goes through the same service (for todos `TodoService::update`, so\n252\t  recurrence keeps working).\n253\t- Actions are **idempotent**: completing an already completed todo succeeds\n254\t  and changes nothing. There is no \"uncomplete\" from the widget.\n255\t- Native flow: tap → explicit, non-exported receiver → the widget shows the\n256\t  new state at once → WorkManager job calls the action → re-fetch all placed\n257\t  widgets. On failure it reverts and shows an error state. Nothing is queued\n258\t  offline; a retry queue can come later if needed.\n259\t- A new action is then mostly backend work, as long as its UI type exists.\n260\t\n261\t### D9: freshness\n262\t\n263\t- WorkManager periodic fetch every 60 minutes, **plus** a one-off fetch at\n264\t  the server's `refresh_at`, **plus** `refresh` from the app after changes.\n265\t  `updatePeriodMillis` is `0`.\n266\t- Work is scheduled in `onEnabled` and cancelled in `onDisabled`: no\n267\t  background work while no widget is placed.\n268\t- **The token never goes into WorkManager input data** (stored unencrypted\n269\t  in WorkManager's database). Workers read it from the token store.\n270\t- The last payload is cached for rendering, excluded from backup, and\n271\t  deleted on `clear`.\n272\t- The widget shows when it was last updated, and tapping its header opens\n273\t  the app.\n274\t\n275\t### D10: dropped\n276\t\n277\tThe first version proposed a long-lived token that native rotates itself.\n278\tD1 replaces it: the widget token lives exactly as long as its session and\n279\trotates with every app token refresh.\n280\t\n281\t### D11: error contract\n282\t\n283\t| Response | Native does |\n284\t|---|---|\n285\t| 401 | Delete the local token, show \"Open solyto to sign in\". The next `ready` asks for a new token. |\n286\t| 404 on a widget route | Show \"Your server doesn't support widgets yet\" (D12). |\n287\t| 404 on an action | The item is gone: re-fetch. |\n288\t| 426 | Show \"Please update the app\" (`RecordClientVersion`). |\n289\t| 429 / 5xx / offline | Keep the last data, mark it as outdated, back off. |\n290\t\n291\t- Native sends `X-Client-Version` on every request, so the existing\n292\t  minimum-version check covers widgets too.\n293\t- The backend gets a `widgets` rate limiter per token and throttles\n294\t  `POST /auth/widget-token`, so a stuck worker cannot flood the server.\n295\t\n296\t### D12: self-hosted and outdated servers\n297\t\n298\t- The app treats a 404 from `POST /auth/widget-token` as \"this server\n299\t  doesn't support widgets\" and does not retry on every start. It sends\n300\t  `unsupported` to native, on the 404 and whenever it skips the request for\n301\t  that reason: without a token, native never fetches and could not find out\n302\t  itself (it would show \"sign in\" instead).\n303\t- Native uses exactly the API URL the app hands over in `setToken`, under\n304\t  the same network security rules as the WebView.\n305\t\n306\t### D13: prerequisite — logout revokes the token on the server\n307\t\n308\t`Auth.logout()` in `../app` calls `POST /auth/logout` before clearing local\n309\tstate. If the call fails (offline), local state is cleared anyway. This\n310\tfixes an existing gap on every platform and is what makes D1's cascade take\n311\teffect on logout. It is its own job and comes first.\n312\t\n313\t## 4. Jobs\n314\t\n315\t| # | Repo | Job | Needs |\n316\t|---|---|---|---|\n317\t| 1 | `../app` | Logout revokes the token on the server (D13) | — |\n318\t| 2 | `../api` | Widget token: issuing, parent binding with cascade, central gate, hidden from the session list (D1) | — |\n319\t| 3 | `../api` | `GET /widgets/your-day`, `todo.complete` action, rate limits, translations (D2, D8, D11) | 2 |\n320\t| 4 | `../app` | Widget bridge module, token requests after `ready`/login/refresh, `clear` on logout, `refresh` after todo/event changes, `navigate` handling, old-server tolerance (D6, D7, D12) | 1, 2 |\n321\t| 5 | this repo | Vendor `../app`; widget foundation and read-only Your Day: message listener, Keystore token store and backup rules, workers, list widget (`res/` layouts, `appwidget-provider` XML with preview, `values-night`, `values-de/fr/es/it` strings for picker text and states), intent handling, error states (D3, D6, D7, D9, D11) | 3, 4 |\n322\t| 6 | this repo | Tap-to-complete on Your Day on the D8 plumbing | 5 |\n323\t\n324\t1 and 2 can run in parallel, then 3 and 4, then 5, then 6. Each job gets its\n325\town backlog entry in its repo.\n326\t\n327\t## 5. Still open\n328\t\n329\t- Whether to also ship static app shortcuts (`shortcuts.xml`) next to the\n330\t  widgets (report open question 7). They are cheap and can use the same\n331\t  route keys.\n332\t- Testing: manual checks per report §7.6. Android test tooling in CI remains\n333\t  a separate decision.\n334\t- Not part of this work: no `sanctum:prune-expired` is scheduled, so expired\n335\t  tokens are never deleted. Worth its own `../api` backlog entry.\n336\t\n337\t## 6. Time tracking widget (2026-10-29)\n338\t\n339\tThe second widget, from the dev request \"Home widgets and NFC activation\"\n340\t(NFC and Samsung Routines are out of scope).\n341\t\n342\t- **Content:** the running timer (project, live elapsed time, stop button)\n343\t  and up to 20 projects, most recently used first, each with a start button.\n344\t  The running project is not repeated in the list.\n345\t- **Starting while another timer runs switches** to the new project in one\n346\t  tap (the server stops the old timer and starts the new one in one locked\n347\t  transaction). Starting the running project is a no-op. Stopping a stopped\n348\t  timer is a no-op. The app's own `POST /time-tracking/entries/start` keeps\n349\t  answering 409 while a timer runs.\n350\t- **No descriptions** from the widget: widgets have no text input.\n351\t- **Format additions**, backwards compatible (`schema_version` stays 1):\n352\t  row `live: \"elapsed\"` (the widget counts up from `at` with a\n353\t  `Chronometer`, without fetching), row `color` (`#RRGGBB`), action type\n354\t  `button` with `icon` (`play` | `stop`), row icon `timer`. `refresh_at` is\n355\t  optional.\n356\t- **Route keys:** `time_tracking`, `time_tracking_project` + project UUID.\n357\t- **Foundation gap found:** job 5 tied the native code to Your Day (one\n358\t  cache, a hardcoded fetch path, Your Day classes referenced throughout).\n359\t  It is made generic first, without visible change.\n360\t\n361\t| # | Repo | Job | Needs |\n362\t|---|---|---|---|\n363\t| 7 | `../api` | `widget-time-tracking`: endpoint, start/stop actions | — |\n364\t| 8 | `../app` | `widget-refresh-on-time-tracking-changes`: refresh after time tracking changes, route keys | — |\n365\t| 9 | this repo | `android-widget-foundation-multiple-kinds` | — |\n366\t| 10 | this repo | `android-widget-time-tracking` (vendor `../app` with job 8 first, by hand) | 7, 8, 9 |\n367\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d08818e-a718-4681-9dcb-edc4bb179adb","uuid":"a109f169-04ea-4ce4-8dfe-018372649d89","timestamp":"2026-10-29T19:50:11.989Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets-decisions.md","content":"# Decisions: Android home-screen widgets\n\ndate: 2026-10-29 (revised the same day, see §1)\nbuilds on: `docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets.md` (2026-10-15)\nscope: architecture decisions for the widget foundation. No code was\nchanged. The first widget is \"Your Day\", but these decisions are meant to\ncarry many more widgets, including interactive ones (tap-to-complete,\nstart/stop timer). The goal is a solid foundation: no choice here should\nneed revisiting when the next widget or the next interaction arrives.\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nThe report recommended a \"web app pushes a snapshot\" design, because it\navoided both the token problem and backend changes. Two facts change that:\ninteractive widgets are a real goal, and the backend can change.\n\nA second pass checked the first version of this document against the code\nin `api/`, `app/` and `android/` and with tap-to-complete in scope from the\nstart. It changed D1, D2, D6, D7, D8 and D9, dropped D10 (token rotation) and\nadded D11–D13.\n\n| # | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| D1 | Native fetches widget data with its **own widget token**, bound to the session token that issued it and only valid on widget routes |\n| D2 | The **backend builds widget data** in one generic, display-ready section/row format |\n| D3 | Native code is **Java + `AppWidgetProvider`/`RemoteViews`**; Glance rejected |\n| D4 | Widgets **show details**; no privacy mode |\n| D5 | **No Capacitor upgrade** is needed for widgets |\n| D6 | Widget taps open routes through the bridge (D7); route keys are mapped to paths in `../app` |\n| D7 | The JS ↔ native bridge uses **`addWebMessageListener`**, restricted to the app's origin |\n| D8 | **Tap-to-complete is part of the foundation**: generic action plumbing, first used by Your Day |\n| D9 | Freshness: periodic fetch, server-given `refresh_at`, refresh after changes in the app |\n| D10 | *(dropped: token lifetime and rotation now follow from D1)* |\n| D11 | One fixed **error contract** between widget routes and native |\n| D12 | **Self-hosted and outdated servers** are handled explicitly |\n| D13 | **Prerequisite:** logout in `../app` revokes the token on the server |\n\n## 2. Findings that shaped the revision\n\nThese were checked in the code on 2026-10-29.\n\n- **Logout never revokes the token on the server.** `Auth.logout()`\n  (`app/src/lib/state/Auth.svelte.ts`) only clears localStorage, and no code\n  calls `POST /auth/logout`. A logged-out token stays valid until it expires\n  (up to 90 days), on every platform. See D13.\n- **`addJavascriptInterface` is visible to every frame**, third-party ones\n  included. The app embeds a Deezer iframe (`DeezerSneakPreview.svelte`).\n  See D7.\n- **Sanctum abilities are not checked anywhere.** Every protected route is\n  behind plain `auth:sanctum` (`api/routes/api.php`), which accepts any valid\n  token. A token with only a `widget` ability would still reach every\n  endpoint. See D1.\n- **The app refreshes its token by itself**: on start, once the token is\n  within 14 days of expiry on native (`shouldRefresh`). The refresh deletes\n  the old token (`AuthService::refreshToken`). See D1.\n- **Mobile users can set their own API URL** (`getCustomApiUrl`), so the\n  server may be older than the app. See D12.\n- **Todo due dates are dates, not timestamps** (`Todo::$casts`,\n  `due_at => date`), and so are all-day events. See D2.\n- **Completing a recurring todo creates the next occurrence**\n  (`TodoService::update`). See D8.\n\n## 3. Decisions\n\n### D1: a widget token bound to its session\n\n- **Issuing:** `POST /auth/widget-token`, called by the web app with its\n  normal token. It creates a Sanctum token named `widget-token` with the\n  ability `widget` and returns it with its expiry. The route must **not** be\n  named `widgets.*` (see the gate below).\n- **Bound to the parent:** a new nullable column `parent_token_id` on\n  `personal_access_tokens`, a foreign key to the issuing token with\n  `ON DELETE CASCADE`, and a **unique** index. Issuing replaces any existing\n  widget token of the same parent in one transaction. So:\n  - there is at most one widget token per session, enforced by the database;\n  - whatever ends a session (logout, revoking it in the session list,\n    `logout-all`, a password change, account deletion, a token refresh)\n    deletes its widget token too, with no extra code paths;\n  - a lost phone is handled by revoking that phone's session, which the user\n    would do anyway.\n- **Lifetime:** the widget token's `expires_at` is the parent's. A widget\n  token can never outlive its session. There is no separate rotation\n  endpoint: every app token refresh deletes the old widget token (cascade),\n  and the app issues a new one right after (D7). The widget secret therefore\n  rotates with the session.\n- **Scope: one central gate, deny by default.** In\n  `Sanctum::authenticateAccessTokensUsing`, a token without the `*` ability\n  is valid only on routes named `widgets.*`. Routes added later anywhere are\n  closed to widget tokens automatically. Normal tokens (`*`) may call widget\n  routes too.\n- **Session list:** `GET /auth/tokens` does not list widget tokens. They\n  belong to their session, and revoking the session covers them. The app's\n  session UI does not change.\n- **At rest on the device:** an AES-GCM key in the **AndroidKeyStore**\n  encrypts the token. The ciphertext, IV, expiry and API URL go into private\n  `SharedPreferences`, excluded from backup (`fullBackupContent` ≤ API 30,\n  `dataExtractionRules` API 31+). That is a manifest change, so flag it in\n  review. `androidx.security:security-crypto` is deprecated; do not use it.\n- Native **never sees the WebView's token**, and never returns the widget\n  token to JS.\n\n**Why:** interactive widgets need server writes from outside the app. A\nseparate token keeps the main credential in one place. Binding it to the\nsession makes revocation impossible to forget or skip, without new revoke\nendpoints or client-side bookkeeping.\n\n### D2: the backend builds display-ready widget data\n\n- One endpoint per widget. The first is `GET /widgets/your-day`, used only\n  by the widget for now. The app's own dashboard Your Day does not change.\n- Your Day content, in this order: **today's events**, **due and overdue\n  todos**, **upcoming events** (the 3-day range `listWidgetEvents` already\n  uses). The \"scored todos\" part of the dashboard stays out: its scoring\n  only exists in TypeScript.\n- One **generic format** shared by all widgets:\n\n  ```\n  {\n    kind, schema_version, generated_at,\n    refresh_at,                         // when this data goes stale (next local midnight)\n    labels: { ... },                    // pre-translated UI strings (title, empty state, ...)\n    sections: [{\n      key, title?,\n      rows: [{\n        id, title, subtitle?, time_label?,  // display-ready text\n        at?, until?, all_day,               // raw values: ISO timestamps, or dates when all_day\n        icon?,                              // key from a fixed icon set, never a URL\n        route: { key, id? },                // mapped to a path by ../app (D6)\n        action?: { type, key, checked? }    // D8\n      }]\n    }]\n  }\n  ```\n\n  - The server knows the user's timezone, language, 12/24-hour and date\n    format, so it produces the text. Native only renders it and cannot end\n    up inconsistent with the app.\n  - Raw values stay for ordering and future use. Dates are never sent as\n    UTC timestamps.\n  - `refresh_at` tells native when to fetch again, so native needs no\n    timezone logic.\n  - **Versioning:** additive changes keep `schema_version`. Native ignores\n    fields, section types and action types it does not know. A higher\n    major version than native supports shows \"update the app\".\n  - The server caps rows per section; the job sets the number.\n- A new widget is mostly a backend endpoint plus a native layout, with no\n  logic rewritten in Java.\n\n**Why:** the API serves every kind of client. Building the data on the\nserver keeps business logic out of Java, and the WebView, the widgets and\nany future client can read the same data.\n\n**Note (optional, not part of this work):** the \"today/overdue\" logic then\nexists both in `TodoFilterService` (TypeScript) and on the server. The\ndashboard's `YourDay.svelte` could later read the same endpoint.\n\n### D3: Java + `RemoteViews`\n\n- The native code is written by AI, so less code per widget is not a deciding\n  factor. What is left favours the platform API:\n  - **No new build tooling.** Glance would bring Kotlin, the Compose compiler\n    and Glance itself, whose versions must stay compatible with each other\n    and with AGP.\n  - **No abstraction layer.** `RemoteViews` is what the launcher renders.\n  - **No F-Droid risk** and no APK growth (release builds run with\n    `minifyEnabled false`).\n  - **One language** in `android/`, matching `MainActivity` and the plugins.\n- Allowed new dependencies, all Apache-2.0, from Google Maven, with no GMS:\n  `androidx.core:core-remoteviews` (list widgets on minSdk 24) and\n  `androidx.work:work-runtime` (D9). `androidx.webkit` is already present\n  (D7). Networking uses the platform (`HttpURLConnection` + `org.json`), with\n  no OkHttp/Retrofit.\n- **Intents:** row clicks in collection widgets need a **mutable** template\n  `PendingIntent` (with an explicit component) for the per-row fill-in\n  intents; Android requires this on API 31+. All other widget\n  `PendingIntent`s are explicit and `FLAG_IMMUTABLE`, with a distinct request\n  code or data URI each. Receivers treat fill-in extras as untrusted and\n  validate the key and id format.\n- API levels: checkboxes (`setCompoundButtonChecked`) need API 31. On\n  24–30, fall back to a tappable icon (report §3.1).\n\n### D4: widgets show details\n\nPlacing a widget on the home screen is the user's own choice, and so is what\nit shows. No \"hide details\" mode and no setup screen for now. A setup screen\ncan be added later when a widget needs per-widget options.\n\n### D5: no Capacitor upgrade for widgets\n\nNothing here depends on a Capacitor plugin. The bridge is our own (D7), and\nwidgets use platform APIs.\n\n### D6: widget taps open routes through the bridge\n\n- Rows carry `route: { key, id? }`, for example `{ key: \"todo\", id: \"…\" }`.\n  **`../app` owns the mapping** from keys to paths and validates the id\n  format. Native keeps no list of paths.\n- `MainActivity` handles widget intents in `onCreate` and `onNewIntent` and\n  only checks the key's format:\n  - **app ready** (warm start): native sends `navigate { key, id? }` over the\n    bridge, and the app calls SvelteKit's `goto()`. An instant switch, with\n    app state kept.\n  - **not ready** (cold start): native keeps the pending route and sends it\n    when the app reports `ready`.\n- The app ignores unknown keys and invalid ids. If the user is not logged\n  in, the normal login redirect applies.\n- `MainActivity` is `exported=\"true\"`, so any app can send it an intent. The\n  worst a forged intent can do is open a known page.\n- Moving share (`handleShareIntent`) to the bridge is possible later but is\n  out of scope.\n\n**Why:** a full `loadUrl` throws away the running app on every tap. One\nchannel for everything avoids a global JS function and building JS strings\nfor `evaluateJavascript`.\n\n### D7: the JS ↔ native bridge\n\n- Mechanism: **`WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener`** with the allowed\n  origin `https://my.solyto.app`. Only that origin's frames get the object,\n  each message carries its source origin and whether it came from the main\n  frame (native accepts main-frame messages only), and native can reply on\n  the same channel. If the WebView lacks the feature, widgets show \"not\n  supported on this device\" and the app carries on.\n- Messages from the app:\n  - `ready`: the app is running. Native replies `{ needsToken }` (true when\n    it has no token or the stored one has expired) and delivers a pending\n    route (D6).\n  - `setToken { token, expiresAt, apiUrl }`\n  - `clear`: logout. Native deletes the token and cached widget data and\n    shows the signed-out state.\n  - `refresh`: data a widget shows has changed (debounced in JS).\n  - `unsupported`: the app's server has no widget routes (D12). Native drops\n    any token and cached data and shows \"server doesn't support widgets\".\n- Messages from native: `navigate { key, id? }`.\n- When the app requests a widget token: after `ready` when native replies\n  `needsToken`, after login, and **after every successful token refresh**\n  (D1).\n- Logout order: `clear` first, then `POST /auth/logout` (D13).\n- In `../app`, all bridge use goes through one `IS_MOBILE`-gated module that\n  checks whether the bridge object exists, so web and desktop are\n  unaffected.\n\n### D8: interactions (part of the foundation)\n\n- A row action is `{ type, key, checked? }`. Native knows only the UI\n  **types** (`check` for now). The **key** names the server action, for\n  example `todo.complete`. Native validates the key format and posts to\n  `POST /widgets/actions/{key}/{id}`.\n- Backend: an **explicit route per action key**, never a generic\n  dispatcher. Each checks ownership the same way the normal endpoints do\n  and goes through the same service (for todos `TodoService::update`, so\n  recurrence keeps working).\n- Actions are **idempotent**: completing an already completed todo succeeds\n  and changes nothing. There is no \"uncomplete\" from the widget.\n- Native flow: tap → explicit, non-exported receiver → the widget shows the\n  new state at once → WorkManager job calls the action → re-fetch all placed\n  widgets. On failure it reverts and shows an error state. Nothing is queued\n  offline; a retry queue can come later if needed.\n- A new action is then mostly backend work, as long as its UI type exists.\n\n### D9: freshness\n\n- WorkManager periodic fetch every 60 minutes, **plus** a one-off fetch at\n  the server's `refresh_at`, **plus** `refresh` from the app after changes.\n  `updatePeriodMillis` is `0`.\n- Work is scheduled in `onEnabled` and cancelled in `onDisabled`: no\n  background work while no widget is placed.\n- **The token never goes into WorkManager input data** (stored unencrypted\n  in WorkManager's database). Workers read it from the token store.\n- The last payload is cached for rendering, excluded from backup, and\n  deleted on `clear`.\n- The widget shows when it was last updated, and tapping its header opens\n  the app.\n\n### D10: dropped\n\nThe first version proposed a long-lived token that native rotates itself.\nD1 replaces it: the widget token lives exactly as long as its session and\nrotates with every app token refresh.\n\n### D11: error contract\n\n| Response | Native does |\n|---|---|\n| 401 | Delete the local token, show \"Open solyto to sign in\". The next `ready` asks for a new token. |\n| 404 on a widget route | Show \"Your server doesn't support widgets yet\" (D12). |\n| 404 on an action | The item is gone: re-fetch. |\n| 426 | Show \"Please update the app\" (`RecordClientVersion`). |\n| 429 / 5xx / offline | Keep the last data, mark it as outdated, back off. |\n\n- Native sends `X-Client-Version` on every request, so the existing\n  minimum-version check covers widgets too.\n- The backend gets a `widgets` rate limiter per token and throttles\n  `POST /auth/widget-token`, so a stuck worker cannot flood the server.\n\n### D12: self-hosted and outdated servers\n\n- The app treats a 404 from `POST /auth/widget-token` as \"this server\n  doesn't support widgets\" and does not retry on every start. It sends\n  `unsupported` to native, on the 404 and whenever it skips the request for\n  that reason: without a token, native never fetches and could not find out\n  itself (it would show \"sign in\" instead).\n- Native uses exactly the API URL the app hands over in `setToken`, under\n  the same network security rules as the WebView.\n\n### D13: prerequisite — logout revokes the token on the server\n\n`Auth.logout()` in `../app` calls `POST /auth/logout` before clearing local\nstate. If the call fails (offline), local state is cleared anyway. This\nfixes an existing gap on every platform and is what makes D1's cascade take\neffect on logout. It is its own job and comes first.\n\n## 4. Jobs\n\n| # | Repo | Job | Needs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `../app` | Logout revokes the token on the server (D13) | — |\n| 2 | `../api` | Widget token: issuing, parent binding with cascade, central gate, hidden from the session list (D1) | — |\n| 3 | `../api` | `GET /widgets/your-day`, `todo.complete` action, rate limits, translations (D2, D8, D11) | 2 |\n| 4 | `../app` | Widget bridge module, token requests after `ready`/login/refresh, `clear` on logout, `refresh` after todo/event changes, `navigate` handling, old-server tolerance (D6, D7, D12) | 1, 2 |\n| 5 | this repo | Vendor `../app`; widget foundation and read-only Your Day: message listener, Keystore token store and backup rules, workers, list widget (`res/` layouts, `appwidget-provider` XML with preview, `values-night`, `values-de/fr/es/it` strings for picker text and states), intent handling, error states (D3, D6, D7, D9, D11) | 3, 4 |\n| 6 | this repo | Tap-to-complete on Your Day on the D8 plumbing | 5 |\n\n1 and 2 can run in parallel, then 3 and 4, then 5, then 6. Each job gets its\nown backlog entry in its repo.\n\n## 5. Still open\n\n- Whether to also ship static app shortcuts (`shortcuts.xml`) next to the\n  widgets (report open question 7). They are cheap and can use the same\n  route keys.\n- Testing: manual checks per report §7.6. Android test tooling in CI remains\n  a separate decision.\n- Not part of this work: no `sanctum:prune-expired` is scheduled, so expired\n  tokens are never deleted. Worth its own `../api` backlog entry.\n\n## 6. Time tracking widget (2026-10-29)\n\nThe second widget, from the dev request \"Home widgets and NFC activation\"\n(NFC and Samsung Routines are out of scope).\n\n- **Content:** the running timer (project, live elapsed time, stop button)\n  and up to 20 projects, most recently used first, each with a start button.\n  The running project is not repeated in the list.\n- **Starting while another timer runs switches** to the new project in one\n  tap (the server stops the old timer and starts the new one in one locked\n  transaction). Starting the running project is a no-op. Stopping a stopped\n  timer is a no-op. The app's own `POST /time-tracking/entries/start` keeps\n  answering 409 while a timer runs.\n- **No descriptions** from the widget: widgets have no text input.\n- **Format additions**, backwards compatible (`schema_version` stays 1):\n  row `live: \"elapsed\"` (the widget counts up from `at` with a\n  `Chronometer`, without fetching), row `color` (`#RRGGBB`), action type\n  `button` with `icon` (`play` | `stop`), row icon `timer`. `refresh_at` is\n  optional.\n- **Route keys:** `time_tracking`, `time_tracking_project` + project UUID.\n- **Foundation gap found:** job 5 tied the native code to Your Day (one\n  cache, a hardcoded fetch path, Your Day classes referenced throughout).\n  It is made generic first, without visible change.\n\n| # | Repo | Job | Needs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | `../api` | `widget-time-tracking`: endpoint, start/stop actions | — |\n| 8 | `../app` | `widget-refresh-on-time-tracking-changes`: refresh after time tracking changes, route keys | — |\n| 9 | this repo | `android-widget-foundation-multiple-kinds` | — |\n| 10 | this repo | `android-widget-time-tracking` (vendor `../app` with job 8 first, by hand) | 7, 8, 9 |\n","numLines":367,"startLine":1,"totalLines":367}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015tUBTuDFCmnHv7SbaZUf9w","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
