{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\nbrief.md\nimplementation.md\nsession.log\ntasks.md\ntokens.json\nverdict.md\n# Brief: widget foundation: support several widget kinds\n\ntype: feature\nid: eat\nbranch: feature/eat_widget-foundation-support-several-widget-kinds\ndate: 2026-10-29\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nMake the native widget foundation serve more than one widget. Today it only\nknows Your Day: one cached payload, a hardcoded `your-day` fetch path, and\n`YourDayPayload` / `YourDayWidgetProvider` referenced from the fetch worker,\nthe action receiver and worker, `WidgetWork`, `WidgetCache` and\n`WidgetBridgePlugin`. After this job, adding a widget means: a kind entry, a\nprovider with its layout, and an API endpoint.\n\n**No visible change:** Your Day must look and behave exactly as before. The\ntime tracking widget ([[android-widget-time-tracking]]) is the first to use\nthis.\n\n## Why\n\nThe decisions (`docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets-decisions.md`, D2/D8)\npromise that a new widget is \"mostly a backend endpoint plus a native\nlayout\". The current code does not deliver that yet.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **A kind registry:** one place listing each widget kind with its `kind`\n   string (as in the payload), its API path (`/api/v1/widgets/<kind>`) and its\n   provider class. For now only `your-day`.\n2. **Generic payload:** rename `YourDayPayload` to a generic name (for\n   example `WidgetPayload`); the format is shared by all widgets. The parser\n   keeps today's behaviour (unknown fields, section keys, icons and action\n   types are ignored).\n3. **Cache per kind** (`WidgetCache`): payload, fetch time, the outdated flag\n   and action state are stored per kind. The overall state (signed out,\n   server without widgets, update the app, device not supported) stays global:\n   it is about the token and the server, not a widget. Keep everything in the\n   one existing `widget_cache` prefs file with kind-prefixed keys, so the\n   backup exclusion rules need no change. On first start after the update,\n   drop the old unprefixed payload keys and fetch again; no migration of the\n   old cache.\n4. **Fetching:** the fetch worker fetches every kind that has at least one\n   placed widget, each from its own path. 401, 404 on a widget route and 426\n   change the global state as today; a 429/5xx/offline marks only that kind\n   as outdated. `refresh_at` is optional per kind (time tracking has none).\n5. **Scheduling:** the periodic work runs while any widget of any kind is\n   placed, and is cancelled when the last one is removed (instead of Your\n   Day's `onEnabled`/`onDisabled` alone). `fetchNow` runs when any kind is\n   placed.\n6. **Actions:** the action fill-in intent carries the kind. The receiver and\n   worker look up the row in that kind's cache, keep action state per kind,\n   and after an action re-fetch **all** placed kinds (a change can show on\n   more than one widget).\n7. **Updating views:** one `updateAll` that re-renders every kind's\n   providers; `WidgetBridgePlugin` (`clear`, `unsupported`, `setToken`) and the\n   workers use it.\n8. **Manual checks:** Your Day as before (data, tap a todo row, tap-to-\n   complete, offline, logout, unsupported server); update from the previous\n   build with a Your Day widget placed (it refreshes and keeps working).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The time tracking widget and the format additions it needs\n  ([[android-widget-time-tracking]]).\n- Any change to Your Day's look or behaviour.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Keep `WidgetTokenStore` as it is: one token serves all widgets.\n- Do not add new dependencies.\nfeature/eat_widget-foundation-support-several-widget-kinds\n# Tasks: widget foundation: support several widget kinds\n\nid: eat\nanalyst: @analyst\ndate: 2026-10-29\n\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\n\nAll code is in `android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/` (`widget/` package plus\n`WidgetBridgePlugin.java`). Nothing in `frontend/`, no manifest, backup-rule,\nlayout, string or dependency changes are expected. There are no Android unit\ntests in the repo (only the Capacitor example tests), so each task is checked\nby compiling (`cd android && ./gradlew assembleDebug`, if the SDK is available)\nand, finally, by the manual checks in TASK-9.\n\nEach task should leave the module compiling: when a task changes a signature,\nit also updates its callers mechanically (for now still with `your-day` only).\n\n## Task breakdown\n\nTASK-1: Add a widget kind registry (e.g. `WidgetKind`) listing each kind with its payload `kind` string, its API path (`/api/v1/widgets/<kind>`), its provider class, a way to re-render that provider (e.g. a `Consumer<Context>` method reference to the provider's own static render-all), and its known section keys; only `your-day` (`today`, `due`, `upcoming`) for now.\n  files: widget/WidgetKind.java (new)\n  depends: none\n  risk: low — new, self-contained file; the only design choice is how a kind triggers its provider's render (keep it a plain method reference, no new base class).\n\nTASK-2: Rename `YourDayPayload` to `WidgetPayload` and make `parse` tak\n\n... [6544 characters truncated] ...\n\nes through static helpers in `WidgetWork`, matching the existing style.\n# Implementation: widget foundation: support several widget kinds\n\nid: eat\ndeveloper: @developer\ndate: 2026-10-29\n\n## Summary\n\nThe native widget foundation now serves any number of widget kinds. A new\nwidget needs an entry in `WidgetKind`, a provider with its layout, and the\nserver's `/api/v1/widgets/<kind>` endpoint. Your Day is the only kind for now.\nIts rendering and behaviour should be unchanged. **This has not been compiled\nor run:** this session had no JDK or Android SDK (see TASK-9).\n\n## Changes\n\nAll files are in `android/app/src/main/java/app/solyto/`.\n\nTASK-1: New `widget/WidgetKind.java`, a public enum. Each kind has its `id`\n(the payload `kind` and the path segment), `path`, provider class, a\n`Consumer<Context>` render reference (the provider's static `updateAll`) and\nits section keys. Only `YOUR_DAY` (`today`, `due`, `upcoming`) exists.\n\nTASK-2: Renamed `YourDayPayload` to `widget/WidgetPayload.java`.\n`parse(data, kind)` checks `kind` against the entry and takes the section keys\nfrom it. Everything else in the parser is unchanged. References were updated.\n\nTASK-3: `WidgetApi.fetchYourDay` became `fetch(context, apiUrl, token, kind)`\nand uses `kind.path`. `postAction` is unchanged.\n\nTASK-4: `WidgetCache` now keeps payload, fetched-at, outdated, action rows and\naction error per kind, under `<kind>.<name>` keys in the same `widget_cache`\nprefs. `state` and `device_unsupported` stay global. `clear` removes every\nkind's keys. The first prefs access removes the old unprefixed keys once,\nguarded by a `per_kind` marker. Nothing is migrated.\n\nTASK-5: Added `WidgetKind.updateAll` (renders every kind) and\n`WidgetKind.anyPlaced`. `WidgetBridgePlugin` and the workers and receiver now\nuse them. I removed `YourDayWidgetProvider.hasWidgets` because nothing uses it\nany more; `WidgetKind.hasWidgets` replaces it.\n\nTASK-6: `WidgetFetchWorker` loops over the kinds that have placed widgets:\n- **401:** clears the token and cache, then ends the run.\n- **404, 426, or a newer `schema_version`:** sets the global state, then ends\n  the run. Any kinds already saved in that run stay saved, but the global\n  state wins.\n- **429, 5xx, offline, or an unparseable payload:** marks only that kind\n  outdated. The worker retries if any kind failed.\n\nThe token-changed check runs after every request. `refresh_at` is scheduled\nper kind under `widget-fetch-at-refresh-<kind>`. `cancelAll` also cancels the\nold unsuffixed name.\n\nTASK-7: `fetchNow` and the fetch worker's early exit now check `anyPlaced`.\n`YourDayWidgetProvider.onDisabled` calls the new\n`WidgetWork.cancelIfNoWidgets`, so work stops only when no kind is placed.\n`cancelAll` is now private.\n\nTASK-8:\n- The checkbox fill-in carries `EXTRA_ACTION_KIND`. The receiver rejects a\n  missing or unknown kind and re-renders, like an invalid key.\n- The receiver looks the row up in that kind's cache and keeps action state\n  per kind.\n- `WidgetWork.runAction` puts the kind into the input data and into the unique\n  work name (`widget-action-<kind>-<id>`).\n- `WidgetActionWorker` treats a missing kind (an action queued by the old\n  build) as `your-day`, so the user's tap still runs. An unknown kind is\n  dropped. After success or 404, `fetchNow` re-fetches all placed kinds.\n- The provider reads its own kind's cache. If the state is `OK` but its kind\n  has no payload, it now shows \"Loading…\" (or \"load failed\" if that kind's\n  fetch failed) instead of \"update the app\". This avoids a wrong message right\n  after the update.\n\nTASK-9: **Not done.** This session had no device, emulator, JDK or Android\nSDK, so I couldn't compile (`./gradlew assembleDebug`) or run any of the\nmanual checks. Someone with a device still needs to do all of them:\n- Your Day shows data\n- tapping a row opens the todo\n- tap-to-complete works\n- offline shows the outdated label\n- logout shows the signed-out text\n- an unsupported server shows its message\n- an in-place update from the previous build, with a Your Day widget placed,\n  refreshes with no \"update the app\" flash\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\n- The code has not been compiled. Run `./gradlew assembleDebug` first.\n- One small change for an edge case: if the state is `OK` but the stored Your\n  Day payload doesn't parse, the widget now shows \"Loading…\" instead of\n  \"update the app\". The fetch worker only saves payloads that parse, so this\n  should not happen in practice.\n- After the update, the widget refreshes only when the launcher sends\n  `APPWIDGET_UPDATE` (usual on a package update), when a periodic fetch runs,\n  or when the app sends `setToken`. Until then it shows \"Loading…\". The manual\n  update check should confirm this.\n- A 404 on any kind's route sets the global \"server without widgets\" state,\n  as the brief asks. This could be wrong for a server that has Your Day but\n  not a newer kind. That is a decision for the time tracking job.\ncat: /workspace/.manigot/manigot.json: No such file or directory","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GF13V8bjDAxabieFw3zqMF"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"973b6541-d4c5-4432-8207-57973f6e2e82","uuid":"61cfabd5-91d9-4a10-8c48-b55efca0b486","timestamp":"2026-10-29T19:55:15.268Z","tool_use_result":"Error: Exit code 1\nbrief.md\nimplementation.md\nsession.log\ntasks.md\ntokens.json\nverdict.md\n# Brief: widget foundation: support several widget kinds\n\ntype: feature\nid: eat\nbranch: feature/eat_widget-foundation-support-several-widget-kinds\ndate: 2026-10-29\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nMake the native widget foundation serve more than one widget. Today it only\nknows Your Day: one cached payload, a hardcoded `your-day` fetch path, and\n`YourDayPayload` / `YourDayWidgetProvider` referenced from the fetch worker,\nthe action receiver and worker, `WidgetWork`, `WidgetCache` and\n`WidgetBridgePlugin`. After this job, adding a widget means: a kind entry, a\nprovider with its layout, and an API endpoint.\n\n**No visible change:** Your Day must look and behave exactly as before. The\ntime tracking widget ([[android-widget-time-tracking]]) is the first to use\nthis.\n\n## Why\n\nThe decisions (`docs/reports/android-homescreen-widgets-decisions.md`, D2/D8)\npromise that a new widget is \"mostly a backend endpoint plus a native\nlayout\". The current code does not deliver that yet.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **A kind registry:** one place listing each widget kind with its `kind`\n   string (as in the payload), its API path (`/api/v1/widgets/<kind>`) and its\n   provider class. For now only `your-day`.\n2. **Generic payload:** rename `YourDayPayload` to a generic name (for\n   